# Week 9 Reflection – PRPL, Service Workers, Offline-First

This week I learned about different cache strategies, and the one that made the most sense to me was **network-first**. 
A network-first strategy tries to get the newest data from the server first. If the internet is not available, it uses the cached version instead. 
I like this strategy because users can still use the app even if their connection is lost.

For our Sprint 3 capstone project, I think network-first would be a good choice for things that change often, like user information, messages, or orders. 
When the user is online, they will always get the latest data. 
If they lose their internet connection, the app can still show the last saved version from the cache. 
This makes the app more reliable and gives users a better experience because it does not stop working right away.

I also learned that **service workers** are what make this possible. 
They run in the background and handle network requests, caching, and offline support. 
Before this week, I did not know how websites could still work without an internet connection. 
Now I understand that service workers and cache strategies work together to make that happen.

Overall, I think this topic will be useful for our Sprint 3 project. 
Using the right cache strategy can make the app feel faster and more reliable. 
It also helps users keep using the application even when they have a slow or no internet connection. 
This lecture showed me that making a good user experience is not just about adding features. 
It is also about making sure the application works well in different situations.
